{"cases":[
{"id":"V3-H05","decision":"R3 critical data mutation, with irreversible-correction risk. Do not run one blanket automated repair. Split the affected rows into three classes. (A) Repair automatically only where the offset can be derived without guessing: rows with an explicit UTC or offset, and local-without-zone rows that fall outside the DST gap or overlap once interpreted in a confirmed zone. (B) Date-only membership boundaries are compared only as calendar dates in the governing zone. They are never converted through midnight UTC. Rows near a boundary are repaired only where a business-confirmed rule and example exist. (C) Rows whose local time falls in the repeated (fall-back) hour, rows in the nonexistent (spring-forward) gap, and rows whose zone or source cannot be determined go to a manual-decision queue. No default offset is applied to them. Nothing is applied until a dry run, reviewed examples, a backup or before-image, and an approval bound to the exact change set are in place.","actions":["Freeze or scope writes to the affected attendance rows, or record a watermark, so the repair does not race with live writes","Take a full before-image or backup of every candidate row, keyed by row ID, and confirm it can be restored","Inventory every timestamp column and its storage semantics (local-naive, UTC, date-only). Record which writer or code path produced each kind, and which IANA zone governs each site or tenant","Use Example Mapping with the business: turn the examples they can explain into rule-bound examples and counterexamples with IDs. Record repeated-hour ambiguity and gap handling as open questions, not as assumed rules","Build a dry-run classifier that labels every row as auto-repairable, boundary-rule-dependent or manual. Output old value, proposed value, the rule or example ID applied and a confidence reason for each row","Use secondary evidence to break ambiguity where it exists, without inventing any: insert order or sequence IDs, audit logs, created_at in UTC, session or schedule records, device events. Rows still unresolved after this go to the manual queue","Have a business owner review a sample of auto-repairs plus all manual-queue rows. Record each manual decision with decider, reason and timestamp","Run the repair idempotently in batches inside transactions, writing an audit table (row ID, before, after, rule/example ID, batch ID, operator, approval ID). Provide a tested reverse script that restores from the before-images","Post-repair verification: run invariant queries for no attendance outside the membership date range, no duplicate or overlapping sessions, and expected counts per day around the transition. Reconcile against the dry-run totals","Root-cause the mixed storage and fix the writers: store an instant plus a zone, or a local date with an explicit zone. Add DST-transition tests built from the example IDs so the data does not re-corrupt"],"required_evidence":["Authoritative governing time zone per site, tenant or record (IANA ID) and the exact DST transition instants involved","Which code path or version wrote each timestamp format, and since when","Dry-run report: counts and row lists per class (auto, boundary-dependent, manual, unrepairable), with the proposed diff","Business-confirmed rules and example IDs for membership date-boundary semantics, including inclusive or exclusive and which zone's calendar date","A recorded business decision per ambiguous row or policy class for repeated-hour and gap cases, or explicit acceptance that those rows stay unrepaired or flagged","A verified restore test from the before-image or backup","Downstream consumers of attendance data (billing, compliance reports, payroll, entitlements) and whether they need reprocessing or notification","Approval bound to the specific dry-run artifact or version"],"risks_or_unknowns":["The repeated-hour local times are genuinely undecidable from the timestamp alone. Any automatic pick of the first or second occurrence is an invented rule","Some local-naive rows may come from a zone different from the assumed one, for example multi-site data or a server-zone default","Converting date-only boundaries through UTC can shift them by one day","Corrected attendance can change derived facts already used externally (invoices, reports, eligibility). Correcting them may be a separate business decision","Live writes during the repair can create rows that are inconsistent with it","The business's 'few examples' may not generalize. Patterns found by the classifier may contradict them","Audit records themselves may contain personal data and need the same handling class"],"stop_condition":"Stop before any mutation if the governing zone is unknown for a row set, if the before-image or restore is unverified, or if there is no approval bound to the dry-run artifact. Stop auto-repair for any row that needs a rule not confirmed by the business, and route it to the manual queue. The repair is VERIFIED only when post-repair invariants and reconciliation pass and every manual-queue row has a recorded decision or an explicit 'left as-is' status. Until downstream consumers are reconciled, report the outcome as UNVERIFIED OUTCOME."},
{"id":"V3-H06","decision":"R3/R4 incident with an external, irreversible side effect. Contain first: keep the worker stopped and do not blindly restart or retry the 12,000 rows. Split the backlog into three groups. (1) Provably not accepted: no request left the system, or there was a definite pre-send failure. These are safe to resend. (2) Provably accepted, per provider evidence. Mark these sent and do not resend. (3) Unknown. For this group, resolve state through provider lookup or reconciliation if the provider actually offers it. Otherwise a business owner decides per message class whether duplicates or drops are worse. The long-term fix is explicit delivery semantics: a persistent idempotency key per message, sent to the provider only if the provider verifiably honors it, and a state machine of pending → sending/in-doubt → sent/failed. Do not assume exactly-once delivery or provider deduplication without documented, tested evidence.","actions":["Containment: pause the worker and any auto-restart, cron or retry policy, and the queue consumers. Record the incident start and end of the partition window","Snapshot the outbox and message table state, plus worker logs, request logs, egress or proxy logs and traces for the window, before any change","Build an inventory of the 12,000 rows by attempt evidence: never attempted, request not sent (connection refused or DNS failure before send), request sent but response lost or timed out, unknown. Include message type, recipient and age","Check what the provider actually offers. Look at the documentation and contract for an idempotency-key header and its dedup window, message lookup by client reference or ID, delivery webhooks or receipts, and exports or delivery reports. Verify each one against a test message, not documentation alone","Reconcile the in-doubt rows against provider-side evidence (status API, webhooks received, delivery reports, provider support export). Move proven-delivered rows to sent with an evidence reference","For rows that remain unknown, get a business or product owner decision per message class on the harm of a duplicate versus a drop: resend, resend with a 'possible duplicate' wording, route to manual or alternate channel, or drop with notification. Bind the decision to the row set","Resend approved rows as a throttled, canary-first batch with monitoring for provider errors, rate limits and complaint or duplicate signals. Keep the ability to halt","Fix delivery semantics: generate a stable idempotency key per message at enqueue time. Persist an 'attempting' or in-doubt state before calling the provider. Pass the key to the provider if dedup is supported and verified. Never auto-retry in-doubt rows without reconciliation. Add a reconciliation job driven by webhooks or the status API","Add tests and fault injection for a lost response after provider acceptance, timeouts and partitions. Add a large-retry circuit breaker or approval gate (a retry above N rows needs a human)","Post-incident: record the timeline, decision record and any user communication. Update runbooks and propagate the learning to tests"],"required_evidence":["The exact partition window, and per-row attempt evidence from logs or traces showing whether the request was actually transmitted","Documented and tested provider capabilities: idempotency or dedup support and window, status lookup by client reference, webhook or receipt coverage","Provider-side records for the window, from the status API, webhook logs or a provider export","Message classification and business harm assessment per type (transactional, security, marketing, legally required), with owner sign-off on the resend or drop policy for unknown rows","Count reconciliation: proven-sent + proven-not-sent + resolved-unknown + accepted-unknown = 12,000","Canary resend results before the full resend","Tests showing the new state machine does not auto-resend in-doubt messages and does deduplicate where the provider supports it"],"risks_or_unknowns":["The provider may have no idempotency or lookup capability, in which case some rows are unknowable and a duplicate-or-drop trade-off must be accepted explicitly","Provider dedup windows may have expired already, so a retry may not deduplicate","Webhook delivery may itself have been lost during the partition","Duplicates of some message types (codes, payments, safety alerts) may be much more harmful than others","Stale messages resent late may be wrong or confusing: time-sensitive content, superseded state","Provider rate limits or abuse throttling during bulk resend","The partition may have affected other side-effecting workers that share the same pattern"],"stop_condition":"Do not restart the worker or resend any row until each of the 12,000 rows is classified with evidence and a business owner has approved the policy for unknown rows. Halt the resend if the canary shows duplicate complaints, provider errors or a mismatch with reconciliation counts. The incident is closed only when counts reconcile, the policy outcome for unknowns is recorded, and the new in-doubt-aware delivery path is tested with fault injection. Report the new semantics as VERIFIED only on test evidence, and as HEALTHY only on production observation."},
{"id":"V3-H07","decision":"R4 under AI governance. Fail closed. A 'read-only' persona is not a control. In its current setup the agent must not process the child and guardian bug reports at all. The approved provider covers source code only, so personal data in prompts, tool output or retrieved issues falls outside the approved boundary. Define a minimum safe execution profile, enforced by the runtime rather than the prompt: repo read only, in an isolated sandbox or worktree; no deploy token or any production credential in the environment; no database MCP; no browser and no general egress; issue tracker either disabled or replaced by a broker that supplies only a sanitized, validated, code-relevant bug summary; output limited to review comments or a diff, with no merge or deploy. Processing personal data requires a separately approved provider or runtime and a documented exception.","actions":["Immediately remove the deployment token from the agent's shell environment and rotate it, because it may already have been exposed in context, logs or the provider. Review its usage logs for unexpected use","Disable the database MCP, browser and unrestricted shell egress for this agent. Run the shell in a sandbox without network access or credentials, limited to the repo worktree","Classify the data: source code is CONFIDENTIAL (allowed for the provider). Bug reports with child and guardian data are PERSONAL/SENSITIVE and involve minors. The deploy token is HIGH-RISK/RESTRICTED. Apply classification to tool outputs and logs as well as prompts","Fill in the AI capability matrix for the reviewer agent. ALLOW: repo read. CONDITIONAL: command exec (sandboxed, no network, no secrets). DENY: repo write outside the worktree, prod logs, prod DB read and write, external messages, merge, deploy staging and prod, secrets/infra, web. Issue tracker: DENY direct access; ALLOW only a broker that returns sanitized fields","Build or choose a pre-model sanitization step for bug reports: extract stack traces, error codes and repro steps, and strip personal identifiers. Validate the redaction on a sample, since redaction does not automatically declassify data. Until that step is validated, a human writes a sanitized summary","Where debugging needs realistic data, use synthetic or fixture data in a non-production environment, never production records","Require human approval, bound to a concrete diff, for any action beyond read and comment. The agent cannot approve its own exceptions","Turn on audit logging for agent identity, model/provider and policy version, tools available, data class processed, and actions and results, without logging the personal data itself","Check whether personal data has already been sent to the provider. If so, escalate to privacy, DPO or legal for incident assessment"],"required_evidence":["Provider policy record: which data classes are verified as allowed (source code yes; personal data and children's data not approved), retention and training terms verified","An inventory of the agent's actual tools, credentials and environment variables, showing the token and the DB MCP, browser and egress are removed (evidence from executable configuration, not prompt text)","Token rotation record and token usage audit log review","Sanitization or redaction validation results on representative bug reports","Tool contracts for the issue tracker broker: fields returned, filtering, output bounds","Historical agent or provider logs showing whether personal data or the token was already transmitted","Named approval owner for any exception allowing personal data to reach a model"],"risks_or_unknowns":["Personal data, including children's data, may already have been sent to an unapproved provider. This may be a reportable privacy incident","The deploy token may already be exposed through model context, logs or prompt injection via bug-report content","Bug reports and issue content are untrusted input. Prompt injection could steer the shell, browser or MCP into exfiltration or mutation","Redaction can miss free-text identifiers, screenshots or attachments","The issue tracker may itself contain personal data in comments, so any direct connector leaks it","Unclear legal obligations for minors' data in the relevant jurisdiction"],"stop_condition":"The agent must not run on production bug reports until the deploy token is removed and rotated, the DB MCP, browser and egress are disabled, and the capability matrix is enforced in configuration and verified. Bug-report content may enter the model only through a validated sanitization path, or after an approved exception for a provider verified for personal data. If prior exposure of personal data or the token is confirmed, stop and escalate to security and privacy incident handling."},
{"id":"V3-H08","decision":"R1/R2: measure before architecting. Reject the summary table and Redis for now, because there is no evidence the join is the bottleneck and both add freshness, invalidation and dual-path complexity. First capture a baseline: p50/p95 for the dashboard's representative scenarios, and an EXPLAIN ANALYZE with buffers on production-like data. Set a performance budget and freshness requirement with the product owner. Then fix the measured bottleneck with the smallest reversible change: missing or wrong index, bad plan or stale statistics, N+1 or over-fetching, unbounded range, pagination. Introduce a new data path only if the measured residual gap still misses the budget after those fixes and an acceptable freshness bound is defined.","actions":["Define the critical dashboard scenarios (typical and largest tenant, date ranges) and get a p95 latency budget and an acceptable staleness bound from the product owner","Capture a baseline: endpoint p50/p95/p99 from APM or logs, DB time versus app or render time, call count per page load (detect N+1), result sizes","Capture EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) for the slow query on production-like data volume. Identify which node dominates: sequential scans, bad row estimates, sort or hash spills, nested loops on large sets","Check statistics freshness or autovacuum, index coverage for join, filter and sort columns, and whether a query or ORM change in the last six months altered the plan","Apply the smallest targeted fix (index, rewritten predicate, bounded date range, pagination, stats refresh). Re-measure against the same scenarios","Only if the budget is still missed: evaluate options in order of added complexity, e.g. a materialized view or rollup with defined refresh, then an application or Redis cache. State the freshness and invalidation contract, expected hit rate and failure behavior for each","Add a regression guard: a performance test or monitor on dashboard p95 with an alert threshold, plus a capacity note on how growth affects the chosen fix"],"required_evidence":["Baseline p50/p95 per critical scenario, and how it changed over the six months","Query plan with actual timings and buffers on representative data","Breakdown of latency (DB versus application versus network versus frontend)","Agreed latency budget and data-freshness requirement","Post-fix measurement on the same scenarios showing the budget is met, or the residual gap quantified","If a cache or summary table is proposed: measured or estimated access pattern and hit rate, the freshness tolerance, invalidation and backfill design, and correctness test plan"],"risks_or_unknowns":["The bottleneck may not be the join at all: N+1 calls, frontend rendering, a lock or contention, or an unbounded range","Plan behavior on production data volume may differ from dev or staging","The freshness requirement is unknown, so any cache or summary table risks showing stale or incorrect data","A denormalized table adds dual-write or refresh consistency risk and an extra data path to secure and operate","Redis cache-hit expectations are unproven. Diverse filters or tenants may give low hit rates and no gain","Continued growth may erode index-only fixes; a capacity projection is needed"],"stop_condition":"Stop investigating and close when the critical scenarios meet the agreed p95 budget on representative data, with before and after evidence and a regression monitor in place. Escalate to a new data path (summary table, materialized view or cache) only when measured evidence shows the budget cannot be met by plan, index or query fixes and a freshness and invalidation contract is approved. If no budget or freshness requirement can be agreed, do not build new infrastructure; record the baseline and the residual issue instead."}
